This folder contains unit tests for Simu5G classes that can be exercised
without a running simulation.

Each *.test file is an opp_test test case: its %activity section becomes the
body of a cSimpleModule::activity() in a one-module network, so the code has a
cComponent to draw random numbers from and can call into libsimu5g directly.

Running the tests
-----------------

    ./runtest                # all tests, against the release library
    ./runtest Foo.test ...   # selected tests
    MODE=debug ./runtest     # against the debug library

or, from the project root,

    make unittests
    make MODE=debug unittests

Both the Simu5G and the INET shared library must be built in the requested
mode, and SIMU5G_ROOT and INET_ROOT must be set (source the setenv script of
both projects). Generated sources, the test executable and the captured
output of each run end up in ./work, which is not under version control.

Writing tests
-------------

Every test is compiled into one shared executable, so anything a test defines
at file scope must either be inline or live inside the test's own %global
section, which opp_test places in a per-test C++ namespace.

lib/ holds header-only helpers shared by several tests; it is on the include
path of every test, but it is not a compiled library and must stay
header-only.

Expected values of the path-loss tests
--------------------------------------

They are not recorded from the model, and they are not written down at all.
Each of those tests reports every data point it checks as a record on its
standard output, naming the formula of pathloss_reference.py -- an independent
transcription of the 3GPP formulas -- that says what the model ought to have
returned:

    #CASE ref=t901_umi_los scenario=URBAN_MICROCELL hBS=10 hUT=1.5 h=20 W=20
          fc=3.5 d3D=21.5 d2D=20 los=1 actual=71.26256854523322 tol=1e-06

A %postrun-command then grades the run and writes a line per case, and a
"graded=N failures=M" summary, to verdict.txt, which the test matches on:

    ./pathloss_reference.py --grade work/Tr38901PathLoss/test.out

So a data point is defined once, by the call in the .test file that produces
it, and there is nothing to keep in sync. The script is given the inputs of a
case and never its output, which is what keeps it an independent statement of
what the specification requires.

Writing a case
--------------

Add the call, name the formula to grade it against, and bump the graded= count
in the %contains section:

    probe.pathLoss("t901_umi_los", m, p, 21.5, 20, true);

To work out what a new case should produce, just run it: the failure line
prints what the specification says it should have been.

The grader evaluates the named formula at those of the reported parameters
that the formula declares. A quantity a report fixes rather than reads -- the
average building height of the TR 38.901 rural scenario, say -- is a constant
inside the transcribed formula and not a parameter of it, so what the test
sets hBuilding to never reaches it. Conversely a parameter the formula
declares and the record does not report is a graded failure, so a renamed
parameter cannot silently go ungraded.

Where the formula has to be evaluated at arguments other than the ones the
model was called with -- a case that checks an argument is clamped, or one
whose result is a difference of two calls -- the test calls record() and
states those arguments explicitly, with a comment saying why.

Checks that have no reference value, because the specification gives no
formula for them, are decided in the test instead: expectRejected() for a
distance outside the validity range, expectValue() for a convention of the
model such as the 1000 dB tolerated-violation sentinel. Those are counted
separately, in the modelchecks= line on stdout.

When the model and the script disagree, the script is right until the
specification says otherwise. Do not adjust it to make a test pass.

The scheduler and BSR tests
---------------------------

The MAC scheduler and buffer-status-report tests (QosSchedulerWeight,
LcgSchedulerFairness, UeBsrReporting, EnbUlBacklog, EnbGrantFolding) consist
of such decided-in-the-test checks entirely, because no specification gives a
formula for what they grade: the QoS weighting of a proportional-fair
scheduler is a modeling decision, and the LCP and BSR rules of
TS 36.321/38.321 are qualitative ("served equally", priority order, report
per logical channel group). What the checks pin down are those qualitative
rules plus the model's own stated contracts -- the byte-for-byte schedule of
a single connection, the invariance of the QoS weight ratio across the QCI
and 5QI priority scales, what a grant is as against what a schedule entry is.
The Checker of lib/SchedulerTestUtil.h prints one verdict line per check and
the "modelchecks=N modelfailures=M" summary the .test files match on.

The classes under test are exercised on bare, never-initialize()d stubs of
the MAC modules they read from (lib/MacTestStubs.h), with connections and
logical channels registered through the real registration API -- so what a
test calls "registration order" is exactly the order bearer establishment
produces in the running model, and a group's membership is the one the
running model would join.

The radio-model tests
---------------------

BlerCqiRoundTrip consists of decided-in-the-test checks too, for the same
reason: no specification says how a simulator should tabulate measured BLER
curves.

It covers the SINR-to-error mapping from both ends -- the curve lookup
(PhyPisaData::getBler) and the CQI the feedback computation derives from it --
as the properties those have to have to mean anything: a BLER is a
probability, a more aggressive modulation is never the safer one at the same
SINR, more SINR never yields a lower CQI, a laxer target BLER never asks for a
slower modulation. It also pins a behaviour that is arguably a defect,
deliberately, so that a refactoring which changes it fails a named check
rather than only a fingerprint: getCqi rounds its SINR while the error model
truncates the same quantity.

Tripwires
---------

Some checks grade nothing about the model: they assert a precondition that
other checks in the same file silently depend on, so that the day it stops
holding is the day a test fails rather than the day a check quietly stops
discriminating. UeBsrReporting asserts that the flat LTE AM and UM RLC header
sizes are still equal, because while they are, its AM figures are also what a
model that ignored the RLC mode would produce. A tripwire's name says so, and
it goes away when the checks it guards become real.

Expected failures
-----------------

A few tests carry an %expected-failure directive. Those record a defect that
has been identified but not yet fixed: they assert what the model ought to
produce, and are reported as EXPECTEDFAIL rather than FAIL until it does. The
%description of each explains the deviation. When one is fixed, drop the
%expected-failure line and fold the checks back into the main test file it
was split out of.
